  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive &middot; banned-verb lint enforced &middot; each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>On the afternoon of September 22, Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi sat down with US Special Envoy Steve Witkoff and Jared Kushner at the United Nations headquarters in New York &#8212; the first structured diplomatic contact between the two governments since the June Memorandum of Understanding expired in July. Qatar&#8217;s Prime Minister and Foreign Minister Sheikh Mohammed bin Abdulrahman Al Thani served as primary intermediary in a three-hour session. Trump described the meeting as &#8220;very good&#8221; and stated a follow-up was &#8220;already planned&#8221;; Witkoff characterized the exchange as &#8220;promising and constructive.&#8221; Iran&#8217;s Foreign Ministry confirmed the encounter and characterized the substance as containing &#8220;nothing new,&#8221; framing it as occurring &#8220;at American side&#8217;s insistence.&#8221; On September 23, Iran tabled a formal 60-day regionwide ceasefire roadmap at the General Assembly, which included phased Hormuz navigation restoration, a freeze on new strike packages, and conditions tied to the release of frozen Iranian assets. → supports <span class="cite">F9</span> without move</p>
      <p>The September 22 UNGA session followed a two-week informal operational lull that emerged around September 8-9, after an acute five-day exchange: US Central Command struck IRGC targets inside Iran on September 1; the IRGC struck US military bases in Jordan, Bahrain, and Iraq on September 2; on September 5 the US struck three Iranian crude oil carriers after the IRGC fired ballistic missiles toward two US Navy warships (Stars and Stripes, AP). Neither side publicly acknowledged the subsequent quiet period as a deliberate pause. IRGC spokesman Hossein Mohebbi stated on September 16 that any new American assault would be answered by a change in &#8220;both the weaponry and the geography&#8221; of the Iranian response. CENTCOM maintained elevated force-protection posture and the JMIC kept the strait advisory at SEVERE through September 24. → supports <span class="cite">F8</span> without move</p>
      <p>Commercial shipping through the Strait of Hormuz remained in the single to low-double digits throughout September: AIS data recorded 7 vessel transits on September 9, 4 on September 15, 17 over the September 20-21 weekend, and 12 on September 22; IMF PortWatch recorded just 1 transit on September 20 against a pre-war baseline of approximately 85 large commercial vessels per day. CENTCOM declared the shipping lanes mine-free on August 28, but the JMIC sustained its SEVERE threat-level advisory, citing drifting uncharted mines and the ongoing hazard from Iranian anti-ship drones and ballistic missiles. Iran&#8217;s September 22 offer of a 7-day Hormuz reopening tied the offer to a precondition &#8212; US lifting the naval blockade &#8212; that Trump refused within hours, stating he would not act until Iran first demonstrated &#8220;sufficient goodwill.&#8221; All three indicators remain NOT_OBSERVED, and with 9 days remaining before the October 3 deadline, no credible near-term pathway to the 30-vessel 7-day rolling average threshold exists. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span></p>
      <p>September 23 Israeli election surveys produced the campaign season&#8217;s sharpest inter-poll divergence: Channel 12 gave Eisenkot&#8217;s Yashar party 23 seats against Likud&#8217;s 20; KAN News showed Yashar 24, Likud 20; Channel 13 showed Yashar 22, Likud 18; i24NEWS/Direct Polls was the outlier at Likud 27, Yashar 22 (Jerusalem Post, Haaretz, September 23). Averaged across four surveys, Yashar held 22.2 seats to Likud&#8217;s 21.2. Bloc-level counts placed Eisenkot&#8217;s opposition at approximately 54 seats and Netanyahu&#8217;s coalition at 50, with neither reaching the 61-seat governing threshold. The Hendel-Zelekha slate and Amcha Israel both tracked near the 3.25% electoral threshold; their presence or absence in the final Knesset could shift several bloc seats without individual party movement. Netanyahu&#8217;s September 24 UNGA address &#8212; previewed as an election speech framing war outcomes as a Likud achievement &#8212; had not yet landed as of this filing. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span></p>
      <p>Horizons on two earlier forecasts passed without resolution: no named Iranian official issued an unconditional public commitment to mediated ceasefire negotiations before September 6 (Pezeshkian&#8217;s September 1 conditional offer fell under the ambiguity rule); no confirmed Iran-US indirect session occurred before September 17 (the UNGA session on September 22 came six days after the deadline). The Knesset&#8217;s natural dissolution on July 17, 2026 &#8212; the first time since 1988 that an Israeli parliament completed its full four-year term &#8212; rendered the Netanyahu coalition-collapse forecast operationally moot, with neither YES branch of its resolution criteria triggered before dissolution; the forecast was retired as OBE today. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board &middot; 1 sentence per scenario</span>
