      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation (40%, &minus;25pp from v48):</strong> F8 resolving NO (38% chance, IRGC breaks the pause) combined with F9 and F10 failing to materialize would return the conflict to active bilateral kinetic exchange; Hormuz at 4-12 vessels/day and all F4 indicators NOT_OBSERVED with 9 days remaining anchor this path in current structural conditions.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted conflict (43%, +15pp from v48):</strong> The informal 14-day operational pause and UNGA proximity talks represent the current operating mode &#8212; a frozen conflict with active diplomatic signaling but no breakthrough; F9 at 48% confirms a follow-up session is more likely than not, but F10 at 11% shows a formal ceasefire framework remains distant.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation (17%, +10pp from v48):</strong> F10 at 11% plus an upside tail in which Iran&#8217;s 60-day UNGA roadmap finds unexpected US acceptance and Hormuz transit recovers; the conditional probability of de-escalation given F9 success and F8 holding is materially higher but requires precondition-unlocking that is not currently in view.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode &middot; 200 words &middot; verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iranian state media in the September 16-24 window ran three simultaneous narrative tracks, each calibrated for a different audience. The first was civic consolidation: state broadcaster IRIB and IRNA gave prominent coverage to Armed Forces spokesman Abolfazl Shekarchi&#8217;s September 16 statement citing &#8220;200 nights of pro-government gatherings&#8221; as evidence of domestic resilience &#8212; framing the war as a shared national burden rather than a leadership failure, directed at audiences enduring inflation above 40% and a rial at historic lows. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
      <p>The second track was nuclear non-negotiability: IRNA and state television covered President Pezeshkian&#8217;s September 23 UNGA address with extensive quotation, foregrounding his declaration that Iran &#8220;will not bow to military action or economic sanctions to give up its nuclear program,&#8221; and presenting the statement as Iran asserting &#8220;legitimate rights&#8221; before the international community. This framing places the nuclear program outside the scope of any deal &#8212; directly bearing on what a formal ceasefire framework could include. → supports <span class="cite">F10</span> without move</p>
      <p>The third track was deterrence-warning amplification: IRGC-affiliated Tasnim and Fars amplified IRGC spokesman Mohebbi&#8217;s September 16 warning that any new US strike would change &#8220;both the weaponry and the geography&#8221; of the Iranian response &#8212; language aimed as much at domestic reassurance as international deterrence, and framing the ongoing pause as a tactical choice, not a concession. → supports <span class="cite">F8</span> without move</p>
      <p>The September 22 meeting with Witkoff was acknowledged by Press TV with the headline &#8220;Iran FM meets US envoy at American side&#8217;s insistence&#8221; &#8212; positioning Iran as responding to US entreaties rather than seeking engagement. No state outlet framed the session as a diplomatic opening or departure from current policy. The aggregate tone placed Iran as a proactive diplomatic initiator advancing a detailed roadmap, not as a party under duress accepting talks. → supports <span class="cite">F9</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

